1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,610
It's Friday, February the
thirteenth, 2004.

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,060
A five-page report arrives by fax at
the Texas parole board and the

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,220
governor's office. A fire scientist has
read the evidence in a death

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:18,330
penalty case, and he is writing to say
most of it doesn't hold up.

5
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,330
The execution is set for Tuesday.

6
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,190
The man on death row is
Cameron Todd Willingham.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,580
Twelve years earlier, a jury found
that he set the fire that killed

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:30,380
his three daughters.

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,820
The fire investigators had told the
jury the burn marks proved that

10
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:37,030
someone poured a liquid and lit it.

11
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:44,600
The fire was two days before
Christmas, 1991, in Corsicana, Texas.

12
00:00:45,470 --> 00:00:50,180
Amber was two. The twins, Karmon
and Kameron, were one.

13
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:52,160
Their father got out.

14
00:00:52,830 --> 00:00:53,640
They didn't.

15
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:59,810
The parole board voted fifteen to
nothing against clemency.

16
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,590
The governor didn't grant a
thirty-day reprieve.

17
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,030
That Tuesday, Texas executed him.

18
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:09,330
In his last statement, he
said he was innocent.

19
00:01:12,130 --> 00:01:16,190
Since then, independent fire
scientists, including one hired by a

20
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:19,920
Texas commission, have concluded the
evidence never showed arson.

21
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,940
But no court has
overturned his conviction.

22
00:01:23,690 --> 00:01:27,750
And the girls' mother, Stacy
Kuykendall, says he confessed to her

23
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:28,660
before he died.

24
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:33,240
Mistakes in capital cases aren't rare.

25
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:37,480
The most careful estimate is that at
least four in every hundred people

26
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,590
sentenced to death in America
were wrongly convicted.

27
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:46,140
So, should a state execute someone
when the evidence is disputed?

28
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,120
Almost every case is
disputed by someone.

29
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:52,340
So when is a doubt serious
enough to stop?

30
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:59,620
We went through the court record, the
fire reports from 1991 and the

31
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:03,740
reviews that came after, the clemency
file, the state commission's

32
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,390
findings, and what the law says
about innocence after a trial.

33
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:11,730
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

34
00:02:23,925 --> 00:02:27,535
On the morning of December the
twenty-third, the girls' mother, Stacy,

35
00:02:27,905 --> 00:02:29,795
went out to pick up a
Christmas present.

36
00:02:30,545 --> 00:02:33,495
Willingham, who was out of work,
stayed home with the children.

37
00:02:34,245 --> 00:02:37,945
His account was that he'd gone back to
sleep, and woke to Amber calling

38
00:02:37,965 --> 00:02:39,695
him and the house full of smoke.

39
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:44,405
He said he tried to reach the twins'
room, his hair caught fire, and

40
00:02:44,425 --> 00:02:45,335
he couldn't find them.

41
00:02:47,695 --> 00:02:50,055
Neighbors saw smoke coming
from the front door.

42
00:02:50,075 --> 00:02:54,415
Flames only appeared when the
children's windows broke, and then the

43
00:02:54,435 --> 00:02:56,065
whole room went up at once.

44
00:02:56,835 --> 00:02:59,305
Willingham was shouting that
his babies were burning.

45
00:02:59,935 --> 00:03:00,925
He had to be restrained.

46
00:03:01,695 --> 00:03:03,625
He was treated in hospital for burns.

47
00:03:06,405 --> 00:03:10,515
Within days, the house was examined by
a deputy state fire marshal,

48
00:03:10,585 --> 00:03:15,415
Manuel Vasquez, and Corsicana's
assistant fire chief, Douglas Fogg.

49
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:19,105
Vasquez had investigated more
than a thousand fires.

50
00:03:19,125 --> 00:03:25,215
He later told the jury: the fire tells a
story; I am just the interpreter.

51
00:03:26,225 --> 00:03:29,105
The state commission would later note
that phrases like that came

52
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:30,795
straight from the training of the day.

53
00:03:33,195 --> 00:03:35,885
What they read in the burned
house was a list of signs.

54
00:03:36,735 --> 00:03:39,985
Shapes on the floor that looked like
puddles of poured liquid.

55
00:03:40,435 --> 00:03:44,275
Burning low down, which Vasquez said
meant the floor was hotter than

56
00:03:44,295 --> 00:03:47,175
the ceiling, and that could
only mean an accelerant.

57
00:03:47,195 --> 00:03:49,605
Three separate points of origin.

58
00:03:50,335 --> 00:03:52,805
Cracked, crazed window glass.

59
00:03:53,295 --> 00:03:56,705
And charring under the aluminum
strip across the front doorway.

60
00:03:57,715 --> 00:04:01,425
Vasquez said the fire had been
intentionally set by human hands.

61
00:04:04,185 --> 00:04:05,615
Samples went to the lab.

62
00:04:06,365 --> 00:04:11,245
One came back positive: the one from the
front doorway, with a residue

63
00:04:11,265 --> 00:04:13,165
consistent with charcoal
lighter fluid.

64
00:04:13,185 --> 00:04:17,985
A barbecue grill had stood on that
porch, with bottles of lighter

65
00:04:18,005 --> 00:04:21,135
fluid, and was moved while the
fire was being fought.

66
00:04:21,845 --> 00:04:25,475
Nothing from the bedroom or the hallway,
where the pour patterns were,

67
00:04:25,685 --> 00:04:26,695
tested positive.

68
00:04:29,415 --> 00:04:32,855
Vasquez also said that if the hallway
floor had been burning, Willingham

69
00:04:32,875 --> 00:04:36,635
couldn't have run out barefoot without
burning his feet, so his story

70
00:04:36,655 --> 00:04:41,515
was a fabrication. A fire scientist
later found his injuries entirely

71
00:04:41,535 --> 00:04:45,195
consistent with being caught
in a burning room.

72
00:04:45,215 --> 00:04:46,725
One thing in his story did change.

73
00:04:47,375 --> 00:04:50,635
He later told his parents he had never
actually crawled into the girls'

74
00:04:50,655 --> 00:04:53,925
room. He said he didn't want people
to think he was a coward.

75
00:04:56,765 --> 00:05:00,605
Sixteen days after the fire, Willingham
was arrested and charged with

76
00:05:00,625 --> 00:05:04,325
murder. The district attorney suggested
on television that the children

77
00:05:04,345 --> 00:05:07,415
had been getting in the way of his
beer drinking and dart throwing.

78
00:05:08,005 --> 00:05:09,475
No motive was ever established.

79
00:05:11,975 --> 00:05:14,095
So, the first weight on the scale.

80
00:05:14,115 --> 00:05:16,825
A fire that killed three children.

81
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:19,645
A father whose story had a hole in it.

82
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:23,605
And an investigator who looked at the
burned floor and saw a crime.

83
00:05:29,784 --> 00:05:34,484
Before the trial, the prosecutor, John
Jackson, offered Willingham

84
00:05:34,504 --> 00:05:36,614
a life sentence if he pleaded guilty.

85
00:05:37,444 --> 00:05:39,514
His own lawyers urged him to take it.

86
00:05:39,904 --> 00:05:43,484
He refused. He said he wasn't going to
plead to something he didn't

87
00:05:43,504 --> 00:05:46,354
do, especially killing his own kids.

88
00:05:47,974 --> 00:05:49,384
The trial took two days.

89
00:05:49,774 --> 00:05:51,724
At its center was the fire marshal.

90
00:05:51,994 --> 00:05:56,094
When the floor is hotter than the
ceiling, he told the jury, that's

91
00:05:56,114 --> 00:05:58,184
backwards, upside down.

92
00:05:58,654 --> 00:06:01,084
The only reason is an accelerant.

93
00:06:03,404 --> 00:06:07,284
The jury also heard from Johnny Webb,
a man in the county jail on

94
00:06:07,304 --> 00:06:11,324
a robbery charge. Webb testified that
Willingham had confessed to

95
00:06:11,344 --> 00:06:15,024
him. Asked about a deal, Webb said
he'd been told no one could do

96
00:06:15,044 --> 00:06:16,074
anything for him.

97
00:06:18,294 --> 00:06:21,734
His court-appointed lawyers consulted
a fire expert, who agreed it

98
00:06:21,754 --> 00:06:23,214
was arson, so he never testified.

99
00:06:23,234 --> 00:06:27,044
They called one witness,
the babysitter.

100
00:06:27,454 --> 00:06:32,064
The jury deliberated for about an hour,
and on August the twenty-first,

101
00:06:33,054 --> 00:06:35,184
1992, convicted him of capital murder.

102
00:06:37,704 --> 00:06:40,994
To decide on a death sentence, the
jury heard about his past.

103
00:06:41,664 --> 00:06:43,534
He had a record for
burglary and theft.

104
00:06:44,124 --> 00:06:47,794
Witnesses said he had beaten Stacy,
including while she was pregnant.

105
00:06:48,604 --> 00:06:52,594
Stacy herself testified for him, and
denied that he'd hurt her.

106
00:06:54,874 --> 00:06:56,764
Then the State called two experts.

107
00:06:57,414 --> 00:07:01,754
One, a psychiatrist named James
Grigson, testified that Willingham

108
00:07:01,774 --> 00:07:06,184
was an extremely severe sociopath who
could not be rehabilitated.

109
00:07:06,674 --> 00:07:09,684
The other pointed to the
heavy-metal posters on his walls.

110
00:07:10,414 --> 00:07:12,384
Neither of them had ever examined him.

111
00:07:13,054 --> 00:07:17,354
Three years later, the American
Psychiatric Association expelled Grigson

112
00:07:17,374 --> 00:07:19,724
for diagnosing people he
hadn't examined.

113
00:07:23,014 --> 00:07:24,784
The jury sentenced him to death.

114
00:07:27,284 --> 00:07:29,414
So, the second weight.

115
00:07:30,244 --> 00:07:33,414
The core of the case was the fire
marshal's reading of the burn marks.

116
00:07:34,164 --> 00:07:38,504
Around it, a jailhouse informant, a
violent record, and experts who

117
00:07:38,524 --> 00:07:41,134
called him a sociopath
without ever meeting him.

118
00:07:47,231 --> 00:07:50,741
For eleven years, his appeals went
through the state and federal courts.

119
00:07:51,331 --> 00:07:54,981
None of them challenged the fire
science; no one had yet.

120
00:07:55,691 --> 00:08:00,351
And the Supreme Court, in a Texas case
in 1993, had said that a claim

121
00:08:00,371 --> 00:08:04,611
of innocence after a fair trial isn't,
on its own, a constitutional

122
00:08:04,631 --> 00:08:10,571
claim at all. The safety net, it said,
was clemency: the fail-safe

123
00:08:10,591 --> 00:08:13,221
in a system run by
fallible human beings.

124
00:08:14,921 --> 00:08:20,781
In early 2004, his lawyer sent the
file to Gerald Hurst, a chemist

125
00:08:20,801 --> 00:08:25,231
with a doctorate from Cambridge who had
spent his career on fire and

126
00:08:26,021 --> 00:08:29,441
explosives. Hurst worked
for free, and fast.

127
00:08:29,461 --> 00:08:32,961
Crazed glass, he wrote, was
an old wives' tale.

128
00:08:32,981 --> 00:08:36,801
A liquid can no more burn under an
aluminum threshold than grease

129
00:08:36,821 --> 00:08:40,051
can burn in a skillet, even
with a loose-fitting lid.

130
00:08:41,061 --> 00:08:45,441
And once a room flashes over, the
whole floor burns, and so-called

131
00:08:45,461 --> 00:08:49,331
pour patterns can't be told
apart from ordinary fire.

132
00:08:51,991 --> 00:08:55,691
On February the thirteenth, the report
was faxed to the parole board

133
00:08:55,711 --> 00:08:56,881
and the governor's office.

134
00:08:57,791 --> 00:09:00,841
The board voted fifteen to
nothing against clemency.

135
00:09:01,311 --> 00:09:05,161
Four of those votes had been cast before
Hurst's report was even signed.

136
00:09:05,991 --> 00:09:07,241
The board didn't hold a hearing.

137
00:09:07,891 --> 00:09:11,491
One of its members later said: we get
all kinds of reports, but we

138
00:09:11,511 --> 00:09:13,441
don't have the mechanisms to vet them.

139
00:09:15,521 --> 00:09:19,231
His lawyers also filed a challenge to
the fire evidence with Texas's

140
00:09:19,321 --> 00:09:22,541
highest criminal court, which ruled
it didn't meet the legal test

141
00:09:22,561 --> 00:09:23,871
for new evidence of innocence.

142
00:09:24,761 --> 00:09:28,221
The governor, Rick Perry, could have
granted a thirty-day reprieve

143
00:09:28,241 --> 00:09:30,291
on his own. He didn't.

144
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:34,341
His office later said he'd been
briefed on the report, and that the

145
00:09:34,361 --> 00:09:36,611
courts had found it was
simply an opinion.

146
00:09:39,941 --> 00:09:45,301
On Tuesday, February the seventeenth,
2004, Texas executed Cameron

147
00:09:45,321 --> 00:09:51,321
Todd Willingham. His last statement began:
I am an innocent man, convicted

148
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:53,271
of a crime I did not commit.

149
00:09:57,271 --> 00:09:58,581
So, the third weight.

150
00:09:59,311 --> 00:10:02,451
A scientist's warning reached the
people with the power to stop the

151
00:10:02,471 --> 00:10:04,501
execution, four days before it.

152
00:10:04,951 --> 00:10:07,201
The law didn't require
anyone to test it.

153
00:10:07,631 --> 00:10:10,521
The Innocence Project found no
record that anyone did.

154
00:10:16,892 --> 00:10:19,262
The science had been
changing since 1990.

155
00:10:19,932 --> 00:10:23,772
That year in Jacksonville, Florida,
investigators were sure a fatal

156
00:10:23,792 --> 00:10:25,602
fire had been set with a liquid.

157
00:10:26,092 --> 00:10:30,052
To check, they burned a condemned
house next door, with nothing but

158
00:10:30,072 --> 00:10:31,342
a couch to start it.

159
00:10:32,072 --> 00:10:36,032
In a few minutes the room flashed
over: everything in it caught at

160
00:10:36,052 --> 00:10:40,372
once. Afterwards, the floor showed the
same pour patterns, without

161
00:10:40,392 --> 00:10:41,982
a drop of accelerant.

162
00:10:42,392 --> 00:10:44,622
The charges in that case were dropped.

163
00:10:46,322 --> 00:10:52,522
In 1992, the year of the trial, fire
investigators got their first

164
00:10:52,542 --> 00:10:56,162
national guide built on the science,
and over the next decade the

165
00:10:56,182 --> 00:10:57,992
old signs fell one by one.

166
00:10:58,542 --> 00:10:59,962
The patterns could come
from flashover.

167
00:10:59,982 --> 00:11:03,592
Low burns, from the heat
radiating down.

168
00:11:04,182 --> 00:11:07,112
Separate patches, from
burning debris falling.

169
00:11:08,122 --> 00:11:12,812
Crazed glass, it turned out, came from
the water sprayed on hot glass.

170
00:11:13,442 --> 00:11:17,562
A Texas commission later said it no
longer had any value as a sign

171
00:11:17,582 --> 00:11:18,032
of anything.

172
00:11:20,532 --> 00:11:24,632
Less than eight months after Willingham's
execution, Texas freed another

173
00:11:24,652 --> 00:11:28,912
man. Ernest Ray Willis had been
sentenced to death for a fire on the

174
00:11:28,932 --> 00:11:30,042
same kinds of signs.

175
00:11:30,772 --> 00:11:34,632
A new prosecutor had the evidence
reviewed, by experts including Gerald

176
00:11:34,652 --> 00:11:39,102
Hurst. They found no evidence of arson,
and Willis walked out of prison.

177
00:11:39,872 --> 00:11:41,962
Same science, same scientist.

178
00:11:42,552 --> 00:11:45,062
Willis had a prosecutor
willing to look.

179
00:11:47,662 --> 00:11:52,762
In 2006, a five-member review panel,
led by fire experts, looked at

180
00:11:52,782 --> 00:11:56,182
the case for the Innocence Project and
found every sign the investigators

181
00:11:56,202 --> 00:11:58,602
relied on had been shown
to be invalid.

182
00:11:58,622 --> 00:12:03,322
Texas had just created a Forensic
Science Commission, and it hired

183
00:12:03,342 --> 00:12:05,692
its own expert, Craig Beyler.

184
00:12:06,302 --> 00:12:07,932
His report was blunter still.

185
00:12:08,982 --> 00:12:12,542
Vasquez's statements, he wrote, were
hardly consistent with a scientific

186
00:12:12,562 --> 00:12:16,352
mindset, and more characteristic
of mystics or psychics.

187
00:12:17,102 --> 00:12:21,002
A finding of arson, he concluded, could
not be sustained, by the standards

188
00:12:21,022 --> 00:12:23,192
of 2009 or of 1991.

189
00:12:25,972 --> 00:12:28,972
Two days before the commission was to
hear Beyler, Governor Perry

190
00:12:28,992 --> 00:12:31,002
replaced its chair and other members.

191
00:12:31,732 --> 00:12:33,242
The new chair cancelled the hearing.

192
00:12:33,852 --> 00:12:38,272
Perry said their terms had expired, and
that it was business as usual.

193
00:12:38,292 --> 00:12:41,672
He called Willingham a monster, and
said witness after witness had

194
00:12:41,692 --> 00:12:43,502
testified to the facts of the case.

195
00:12:44,432 --> 00:12:47,732
The chair he replaced later described
an endless drumbeat of efforts

196
00:12:47,752 --> 00:12:49,222
to stop the investigation.

197
00:12:51,922 --> 00:12:55,232
The State Fire Marshal's Office said it
stood by the original findings.

198
00:12:55,942 --> 00:13:00,082
In 2011, the commission called that
position untenable in light of

199
00:13:00,102 --> 00:13:04,302
the science. It found that the key
signs had lost their value, and

200
00:13:04,322 --> 00:13:08,072
that neither the State Fire Marshal's
Office nor the city's fire department

201
00:13:08,382 --> 00:13:11,472
had told the courts or prosecutors
that the standards had changed.

202
00:13:12,202 --> 00:13:15,402
But it made no finding of negligence,
and said plainly that it was

203
00:13:15,422 --> 00:13:18,092
making no comment on anyone's
guilt or innocence.

204
00:13:19,202 --> 00:13:22,422
The Attorney General then ruled that it
had no power to look at evidence

205
00:13:22,442 --> 00:13:24,472
from before 2005 at all.

206
00:13:26,892 --> 00:13:28,902
The informant changed his story too.

207
00:13:29,632 --> 00:13:33,202
Johnny Webb tried to recant in
2000, then withdrew it.

208
00:13:33,652 --> 00:13:37,362
In 2014 he said he'd lied in
exchange for help.

209
00:13:37,952 --> 00:13:41,712
Letters showed the prosecutor, John
Jackson, had written on Webb's

210
00:13:41,732 --> 00:13:45,902
behalf after the trial, and an unsigned
note in the prosecutor's file,

211
00:13:46,452 --> 00:13:51,042
the Innocence Project says, linked Webb's
reduced charge to his testimony.

212
00:13:52,192 --> 00:13:57,252
Jackson denied any deal, and in 2017, a
jury in the State Bar's case

213
00:13:57,272 --> 00:14:01,502
against him found, eleven to one, that
he had not committed misconduct.

214
00:14:04,062 --> 00:14:07,232
His family asked for a court to
declare him wrongly convicted.

215
00:14:07,842 --> 00:14:10,042
That inquiry was stopped
before it ruled.

216
00:14:10,062 --> 00:14:13,972
In 2014, they asked for a
posthumous pardon.

217
00:14:14,642 --> 00:14:16,032
The parole board said no.

218
00:14:16,522 --> 00:14:17,692
His conviction stands.

219
00:14:20,232 --> 00:14:22,422
Texas has since changed some rules.

220
00:14:22,972 --> 00:14:26,332
The commission recommended that when the
science changes, investigators

221
00:14:26,352 --> 00:14:29,242
have a responsibility to tell
the people affected.

222
00:14:29,952 --> 00:14:34,392
In 2013, Texas let courts overturn a
conviction if new science shows

223
00:14:34,412 --> 00:14:36,522
the person likely wouldn't
have been convicted.

224
00:14:37,272 --> 00:14:39,302
It came nine years too
late for Willingham.

225
00:14:39,952 --> 00:14:43,512
England took a different road long
before: it abolished the death

226
00:14:43,532 --> 00:14:45,542
penalty for murder in 1965.

227
00:14:48,242 --> 00:14:49,472
So, the fourth weight.

228
00:14:50,002 --> 00:14:53,612
Every independent review found the
fire evidence didn't show arson.

229
00:14:54,262 --> 00:14:56,072
The State has never said it was wrong.

230
00:14:56,822 --> 00:14:59,762
And the system that might have tested
that, before the execution,

231
00:14:59,782 --> 00:15:01,632
didn't exist yet.

232
00:15:07,768 --> 00:15:10,318
First, the strongest case
for the State.

233
00:15:11,088 --> 00:15:15,008
The conviction never rested on the fire
science alone, its defenders

234
00:15:15,028 --> 00:15:19,908
say: there was the informant, the
neighbors' accounts, and a man with

235
00:15:19,928 --> 00:15:24,828
a violent past. His conviction was
reviewed by court after court for

236
00:15:24,848 --> 00:15:30,128
eleven years. Fire investigators in
1991 did what they'd been trained

237
00:15:30,148 --> 00:15:33,858
to do, and his own lawyers couldn't
find an expert to say otherwise.

238
00:15:34,448 --> 00:15:38,428
And the person who knew him best, the
girls' mother, says he confessed

239
00:15:38,448 --> 00:15:44,328
to her. In 1992, and again years
later, she said she didn't believe

240
00:15:44,348 --> 00:15:48,768
he did it; she says now that just
before he was executed, he told

241
00:15:48,788 --> 00:15:49,818
her he did it.

242
00:15:52,138 --> 00:15:54,088
Now the strongest case against.

243
00:15:54,578 --> 00:15:58,258
The only physical evidence that a
crime happened at all didn't show

244
00:15:58,278 --> 00:16:02,858
arson. What remained was an informant who
has told at least four versions

245
00:16:02,878 --> 00:16:07,068
of his story, and experts who
diagnosed a man they'd never met.

246
00:16:07,878 --> 00:16:11,778
A credible scientist warned the people
who could stop the execution,

247
00:16:11,798 --> 00:16:16,338
four days ahead, and nothing required
them to test what he said.

248
00:16:16,358 --> 00:16:20,148
Whether Willingham set the fire, no
one can now prove either way.

249
00:16:20,838 --> 00:16:25,278
But that's the point: the State executed
him on evidence that no independent

250
00:16:25,298 --> 00:16:26,748
review has stood behind.

251
00:16:29,428 --> 00:16:30,118
Here's our verdict.

252
00:16:30,848 --> 00:16:34,368
On the fire evidence: the signs the
jury heard were not evidence of

253
00:16:34,388 --> 00:16:38,508
arson, by today's science or by the
best fire science of 1991.

254
00:16:38,528 --> 00:16:42,468
Our confidence is ninety percent.

255
00:16:42,488 --> 00:16:46,548
What would change our mind: an
independent fire scientist defending

256
00:16:46,568 --> 00:16:50,968
those signs on modern science, or lab
results showing accelerant where

257
00:16:50,988 --> 00:16:51,938
the patterns were.

258
00:16:55,998 --> 00:17:00,698
On February the seventeenth, 2004:
Texas should not have carried out

259
00:17:00,718 --> 00:17:02,128
the execution that day.

260
00:17:02,558 --> 00:17:05,898
With a credible scientific challenge
to its only physical evidence

261
00:17:05,918 --> 00:17:09,638
on the table, it should have used the
thirty-day reprieve to test

262
00:17:09,658 --> 00:17:13,178
it. Our confidence is
eighty-five percent.

263
00:17:13,198 --> 00:17:18,828
What would change our mind: records
showing a qualified fire scientist

264
00:17:19,418 --> 00:17:24,538
reviewed Hurst's report for the State
before the execution, and found

265
00:17:24,558 --> 00:17:25,808
it wrong on the merits.

266
00:17:30,088 --> 00:17:35,188
And on the question: no state should
carry out an execution while

267
00:17:35,208 --> 00:17:39,088
a credible, specific challenge to the
evidence that convicted the

268
00:17:39,108 --> 00:17:42,998
person hasn't been heard by
someone competent to judge it.

269
00:17:43,788 --> 00:17:46,488
Our confidence is eighty percent.

270
00:17:46,508 --> 00:17:50,188
What would change our mind: evidence
that a rule like that is routinely

271
00:17:50,208 --> 00:17:53,818
used to delay executions
without finding real errors.

272
00:17:54,088 --> 00:17:58,068
As for whether he set the fire: we
can't know, and nobody can now

273
00:17:58,088 --> 00:17:59,118
prove it either way.

274
00:18:02,778 --> 00:18:03,508
That's our verdict.

275
00:18:04,098 --> 00:18:06,908
It's open, which means it can change.

276
00:18:07,658 --> 00:18:10,628
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

277
00:18:11,118 --> 00:18:13,448
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

278
00:18:16,838 --> 00:18:20,048
Next case: Berkeley, 1969.

279
00:18:21,258 --> 00:18:24,778
A graduate student tells his university
therapist he intends to kill

280
00:18:24,798 --> 00:18:29,148
a young woman. The therapist asks the
campus police to detain him.

281
00:18:29,658 --> 00:18:31,608
They hold him briefly, and let him go.

282
00:18:32,278 --> 00:18:33,648
No one warns her family.

283
00:18:34,378 --> 00:18:36,088
That October, he kills her.

284
00:18:36,798 --> 00:18:40,918
Her parents sue, and California's
Supreme Court rules that a therapist

285
00:18:40,938 --> 00:18:44,448
can owe a duty to protect the
person a patient threatens.

286
00:18:45,458 --> 00:18:48,678
Should a therapist break a patient's
confidence to warn someone in

287
00:18:48,698 --> 00:18:49,208
danger?
